<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Caithness</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Caithness is a historic county in the far north-east of mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, bounded by the Pentland Firth and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>. It is rich in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> brochs and was part of Pictish territory before Norse settlers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a> took it over in the 9th century; the earls of Orkney later held it as a Scottish earldom. Its towns include Wick and Thurso, and its coast ends at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duncansby-head">Duncansby Head</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunnet-head">Dunnet Head</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/caithness.jpg" alt="Caithness" width="320" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Visitor from Wikishire. This particular view by Inops · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caithness-Scotland.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Caithness extends about 48 kilometres north to south and about the same east to west. Unlike most of the north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> its landscape is generally flat, with open farmland, moorland and scattered settlements, and until the late 20th century it had almost no woodland. Much of the interior is the Flow Country, the largest expanse of blanket bog in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Dunnet Head is the northernmost point of mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duncansby-head">Duncansby Head</a>, near John o' Groats, its north-eastern tip. Most of the rock is Old Red Sandstone, which splits into flagstone and has been used for building since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> times.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The county holds the remains of prehistoric occupation, among them the Grey Cairns of Camster, the Stone Lud and over 100 brochs. It was anciently part of the Pictish kingdom of Cat, which also included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutherland">Sutherland</a>, and the Caith element of the name comes from the Cat or Catti people, while the ending is Old Norse for headland. In the 9th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigurd-eysteinsson">Sigurd Eysteinsson</a>, Jarl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>, conquered it, and his successors owed allegiance to the Norwegian crown. The Scottish crown claimed overlordship from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> in 1098. Many place names and surnames, such as Gunn, are Norse in origin, and Norn was the everyday language until Scots and then English overtook it.</p><p>The Diocese of Caithness was founded in the 12th century, with its seat first at Halkirk and in the early 13th century at Dornoch Cathedral. Caithness became a separate earldom in the 14th century, and David Stewart, Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strathearn">Strathearn</a>, was made Earl of Caithness between 1375 and 1377. A shire covering the earldom was created in 1641, with Wick as its head burgh. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobite-rising-of-1745">Jacobite rising of 1745</a> the Heritable Jurisdictions (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>) Act 1746 ended the hereditary sheriffdom held by the Earl of Caithness. The county ceased to be used for local government in 1975, when it became part of the Highland region.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Caithness has two towns, Wick, which was the county town, and Thurso. Ferries across the Pentland Firth link it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>, and Wick has an airport. The old county boundaries are still used as a registration county.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Caithness” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A later medieval tract names seven provinces, Cait (<b>Caithness</b>), Ce, Fib (Fife), Fidach, Fotla (Atholl), Circinn and Fortriu, and a list of Pictish kings survives in later copies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-picts">Kingdom of the Picts · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Vikings conquered and settled the islands and mainland areas such as <b>Caithness</b> and Sutherland, and in a major battle in 839 they killed the King of Fortriu, Eógan mac Óengusa, the King of Dál Riata, Áed mac Boanta, and many others.</blockquote><a href="/culture/picts">Picts · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>The name, perhaps 'cat people', survives in <b>Caithness</b>, the Norse 'headland of the Cats', and Gaelic Cataibh, the name for Sutherland.</blockquote><a href="/state/cait">Cait</a></li><li><blockquote>Ptolemy's Geography of about 150 places the Cornavii in the far north-east of mainland Scotland, in modern <b>Caithness</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/cornavii-caithness">Cornavii (Caithness)</a></li><li><blockquote>The earls owed allegiance to the kings of Norway, and later also to the kings of Scotland for <b>Caithness</b>, but often acted independently.</blockquote><a href="/state/earldom-of-orkney">Earldom of Orkney</a></li></ul></section>
